
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After Apple announced the iPhone Duo, I had a spare half hour before a MacSparky Labs meetup about the new announcements. On a whim, I turned on the camera and talked through my thoughts on the new device. I posted the video a few days later without expecting much, but for whatever reason, the YouTube gods loved it. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gW77mX5XRwo">That little video</a> now has over a million views.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Between <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/fieldguides/">the Field Guides</a> and the MacSparky Labs, I&#8217;ve made thousands of videos. Very few of them get published publicly on YouTube, but I&#8217;ve been trying to do that a bit more lately. I&#8217;d like to hopefully get &quot;found&quot; by a few more like-minded people who may want to <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/newsletter/">sign up for the newsletter</a> or <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/join/">the Labs</a>. I&#8217;ve also got a few things I want to get off my chest.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This unexpected viral video gave me a chance to see the impact of a million views on the MacSparkyverse. The answer, honestly, is not much. I didn&#8217;t see much change in newsletter subscriptions, Labs subscriptions, or Field Guide sales. It did add a few more subscribers to my YouTube channel. I feel like that&#8217;s good to know.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Granted, this video was just me talking about the new iPhone, and I didn&#8217;t make any pitch for people to buy anything or sign up. That makes this a pretty limited test of what YouTube can do for the business. Still, I think it&#8217;s a good data point. It doesn&#8217;t change my opinion that I should be making a few public YouTubes every month, and I intend to do so.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There&#8217;s also another piece of this. We all have to deal with metrics, whether you&#8217;re a doctor, a plumber, or a silly nerd making videos about iPhones. It&#8217;s very easy for us to let those metrics take the wheel. I can tell you that after my big successful video, there was a part of me … just for a minute … that thought, &quot;Hey Sparky, how can you make every video a million-view video?&quot; The answer is to start letting the algorithm drive and planning content around views rather than whatever it is I actually want to say.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fortunately, I didn&#8217;t think about that too long. I&#8217;m not looking to become a world-famous YouTuber here. This is just another little thing I want to do in addition to the rest of the things I do as MacSparky, and by no means is it the most important. It&#8217;s a good reminder that it&#8217;s very easy to turn views, praise, rankings, or other metrics in on themselves and let them quietly displace the purpose of the work.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For me, I&#8217;m always going to ask myself, &quot;Would I still want to make this video if it got my usual number of views?&quot; before I&#8217;ll ask myself, &quot;Is this topic going to blow the roof off YouTube?&quot; I bet you&#8217;ve got a similar metric that could easily derail you. What&#8217;s your test to make sure you don&#8217;t fall into that trap?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">P.S. Here&#8217;s <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FWaAbJO6BHQ">my most recent one, talking about where we should draw our lines with AI</a>. You&#8217;ll see that one&#8217;s doing just fine, but isn&#8217;t anywhere near the success of the iPhone Duo video.</p>
